There is a state desynchronization issue here. When `selectedSessionId` is `undefined` or belongs to another repository, `activeSessionId` falls back to the first session (`sessions[0]?.id_ref`) for visual highlighting in the sidebar. However, the actual `selectedSessionId` state in the parent component remains `undefined` or outdated. As a result, the main Workspace area will not load or display the highlighted session's content until the user explicitly clicks on it, leading to a confusing user experience.

To resolve this, use a `useEffect` hook to synchronize the `selectedSessionId` state with the first session of the newly selected repository when the current ID is invalid or missing.

```
  const sessions = selectedRepo === undefined
    ? []
    : status.opencode_sessions.sessions.filter((session) => session.repo_path_ref === selectedRepo.path_ref);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (sessions.length > 0 && !sessions.some((session) => session.id_ref === selectedSessionId)) {
      setSelectedSessionId(sessions[0].id_ref);
    }
  }, [sessions, selectedSessionId, setSelectedSessionId]);

  const activeSessionId = sessions.some((session) => session.id_ref === selectedSessionId) ? selectedSessionId : sessions[0]?.id_ref;
```

