![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The current implementation of `ansiClassForCodes` resets the active color to an empty string whenever a non-color ANSI code (such as `1` for bold) is encountered. This causes active colors to be lost prematurely. Additionally, it doesn't correctly handle sequences that combine reset and color codes (e.g., `0;31` or `31;0`) in the correct order.

We can resolve this by passing the current `activeClass` as a second argument and iterating through the codes from left to right to update the state sequentially.

```
function ansiClassForCodes(rawCodes: string, currentClass: string): string {
  const codes = rawCodes.length === 0 ? [0] : rawCodes.split(";").map((code) => Number.parseInt(code, 10));
  let active = currentClass;
  for (const code of codes) {
    if (code === 0) {
      active = "";
    } else if ((code >= 30 && code <= 37) || (code >= 90 && code <= 97)) {
      active = "ansi-fg-" + code;
    }
  }
  return active;
}
```

