The runAction function performs a network request using fetch but does not check if the response was successful (response.ok) before parsing it as JSON. If the server returns an error status code (such as 500 Internal Server Error or 404 Not Found), response.json() may fail to parse or return an envelope without the expected data property, leading to runtime crashes or unhandled promise rejections. Additionally, network failures (such as offline or DNS issues) will throw an error that is not caught. We should wrap the fetch call in a try-catch block and verify response.ok before attempting to parse and use the response data.

```
  async function runAction(): Promise<void> {
    if (disabled) {
      return;
    }

    try {
      const response = await fetch('/api/apps/' + encodeURIComponent(app.id) + '/actions/' + action, { method: 'POST' });
      if (!response.ok) {
        console.error('Failed to run action ' + action + ': ' + response.statusText);
        return;
      }
      const envelope = await response.json() as GuiResponseEnvelope<GuiAppActionJobSummary>;
      if (envelope.ok && envelope.data) {
        onJob(envelope.data);
      }
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Network error running action ' + action + ':', error);
    }
  }
```

