![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Sharing a stateful `RegExp` instance (using the `g` flag) at the module scope can lead to race conditions or unexpected behavior if the function is ever called concurrently, re-entrantly, or in an asynchronous context.

While the original code used `new RegExp(..., 'g')` which compiles the regex at runtime, modern JavaScript engines optimize regex literals (e.g., `/pattern/g`) by compiling them once at load time. Therefore, defining a local regex literal inside the function is both highly performant and completely safe from shared-state bugs.

```suggestion
  const ansiPattern = /\x1b\[([0-9;]*)m/g;
  let activeClass = "";
  let lastIndex = 0;
  let match = ansiPattern.exec(line);

  while (match !== null) {
    if (match.index > lastIndex) {
      nodes.push(terminalSpan(line.slice(lastIndex, match.index), activeClass, `${keyPrefix}-${nodes.length}`));
    }
    activeClass = ansiClassForCodes(match[1]);
    lastIndex = ansiPattern.lastIndex;
    match = ansiPattern.exec(line);
