If `row.usage` is `undefined` (which is common for events without model usage), `row.usage === null` evaluates to `false`. This causes a runtime `TypeError` when attempting to access `row.usage.total_tokens` in the fallback branch. Using a loose nullish check or `!row.usage` prevents this crash.

```suggestion
function resourceSummary(row: PulseEvent): string {
  const model = row.usage?.model_ref?.replace("model:", "");
  const providerLabel = row.usage?.provider === "openai" ? "OpenAI" : row.usage?.provider === "anthropic" ? "Anthropic" : row.usage?.provider;
  const provider = !row.usage ? row.resources[0]?.available_label ?? "metadata only" : `${providerLabel ?? "Provider"} · ${model ?? "model metadata pending"}`;
  const tokens = !row.usage ? "" : ` · ${row.usage.total_tokens.toLocaleString()} tokens`;
  const cost = row.usage?.estimated_cost_ref == null ? "" : ` · ${row.usage.estimated_cost_ref}`;

  return `${provider}${tokens}${cost}`;
}
```

![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

Similar to `resourceSummary`, if `event.usage` is `undefined`, checking `event.usage === null` is insufficient and will lead to a runtime crash when accessing `event.usage.provider` or `event.usage.total_tokens`. Using `!event.usage` handles both `null` and `undefined` safely.

```suggestion
function usageSummary(event: PulseEvent): string {
